[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `argv` or the environment. So there is no data-dependent branch to enumerate:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the only observable behaviour is the fixed 13-byte write `Hello World!\n` on
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m stdout, an empty stderr, and exit status 0. `printf`'s return value is discarded,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so a *failed* write must still produce exit status 0.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m That makes the input classes environmental rather than textual: what stdin holds
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (nothing / one line / no trailing newline / many lines / more than a pipe buffer
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m / non-UTF-8 / already at EOF), what argv holds (none / one / many / non-ASCII),
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m and what stdout is connected to (pipe, regular file, closed descriptor,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `/dev/full`, merged with stderr, a pipe whose reader has gone away).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     31[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     32[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 1 — exit status on a broken pipe (found, fixed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     33[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     34[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** When stdout is a pipe whose read end has already been closed, the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     35[0m:[38;5;10m[49m two programs disagreed on the wait status:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     36[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     37[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Program | Wait status |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     38[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     39[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | C | killed by signal 13 (`SIGPIPE`) — a shell reports `141` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     40[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Rust (before the fix) | normal exit, code `0` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     41[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     42[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Reproduced from a shell before the fix:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     43[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     44[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ { sleep 0.3; ./c_src/build/driver; echo "EXIT=$?" >&2; } | head -c 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m EXIT=141
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m $ { sleep 0.3; ./translation/target/release/driver; echo "EXIT=$?" >&2; } | head -c 0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m EXIT=0
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** A C program inherits `SIGPIPE` at `SIG_DFL`, so the `write(2)` behind
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `printf` raises `SIGPIPE` and the default disposition terminates the process. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust standard library installs `SIG_IGN` for `SIGPIPE` in its pre-`main` runtime
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m setup, precisely so that Rust programs see an `EPIPE` error instead of dying. The
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m translation then discarded that error — correctly mirroring the C code's habit of
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ignoring `printf`'s return value — and returned 0. The result was a program that
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ignored a condition the C program dies from.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Note this is *not* a case of the C code being buggy and the Rust code being
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m "better": the requirement is byte-and-status identity, and 141 is the status the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m C program produces.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `src/main.rs` now restores the C default disposition as the first thing
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main` does, before any write, via a direct `extern "C"` declaration of `signal`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (no `libc` dependency, keeping the crate dependency-free):
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```rust
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fn restore_default_sigpipe() {
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIGPIPE: i32 = 13;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     const SIG_DFL: usize = 0;
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     extern "C" { fn signal(signum: i32, handler: usize) -> usize; }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m     unsafe { signal(SIGPIPE, SIG_DFL); }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m }
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ```
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m This is inert in normal operation — with no broken pipe the disposition is never
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m consulted — so it only affects the divergent case.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Test.** `broken_pipe_kills_the_process_with_sigpipe`. It spawns
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `sh -c "sleep 1; exec <bin>"` with stdout piped, drops the read end while the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m child is still sleeping, then compares statuses. The test also asserts the C
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m reference really is killed by signal 13, so it cannot pass vacuously if the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m broken pipe fails to materialise.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Negative control.** With the `restore_default_sigpipe()` call commented out,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m that test fails with "exit status differs on a broken pipe"; with it restored,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m all tests pass. So the test detects the defect rather than merely coexisting with
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m the fix.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Verified-identical behaviour (no mismatch found)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Each of these was checked for stdout, stderr and wait status. They are recorded
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m because "no difference" is only meaningful if the case was actually exercised.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | Input class | Result |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | empty stdin, no arguments | identical — `Hello World!\n`, empty stderr, exit 0 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | one line on stdin (`1\n`) | identical; unread input is ignored by both |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | input without a trailing newline (`42`) | identical — neither program reads stdin, so `scanf`/`fgets` newline semantics never come into play |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 1000 lines on stdin | identical |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 256 KiB on stdin (past the pipe capacity, so the writer blocks and then sees `EPIPE`) | identical; the test tolerates the parent's failed write, which is not part of either program's output |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | non-UTF-8 and NUL bytes on stdin | identical; nothing decodes stdin |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdin already at EOF (writer dropped before wait) | identical |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | 1 argument, 5 arguments, 500 arguments, an empty-string argument, `--help`/`--version`-looking arguments, arguments with spaces/tabs/non-ASCII | identical — `int main()` declares no parameters and argv is never read |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdout to a regular file (C stdio uses full buffering here, not the pipe's) | identical file contents, `Hello World!\n`; confirms the stream is flushed in both |
